Overall: 79/219 (36%) of Level-2 perturbation tests flipped process_anomaly_score back to >= 0.5 (the AS-BUILT-correct direction for this project's trust-style scale -- see this script's module docstring). Target (Section C.4, [21]'s method): >= 70%. DOES NOT YET MEET the target.

WHY the lstm_ae_score row is 0% -- this is a property of the METRIC applied to
multi-channel physical anomalies, not an untrained model or a coding defect, and
it was measured rather than assumed:
  * The Level-2 procedure repairs exactly ONE feature channel, and the anomaly
    does not live in one channel. Measured below on real hardware: repairing the
    best single channel sharply reduces reconstruction error but does not reach
    the score threshold; the measured errors and required cutoff are reported below.
  * Repairing THREE channels together clears the threshold for the large majority
    of windows; the exact count and most common repair sets are reported below. Those
    three are all amplitude functions of the same spike (crest_factor IS
    peak/rms), so no one of them can carry the repair alone. The most common
    sufficient sets and their observed counts are reported below --
    attribution names the most diagnostic channel, repair needs the sufficient
    set, and they are different questions.
  * The ATTRIBUTION remains sound and useful: kurtosis is the channel most
    often named, the physically correct answer for an impulsive spike.
  The flip test is a fair pass/fail for a point model (see gnn_score at 100%), but
  for a sequence model on a correlated multi-channel event it asks the model to
  undo an anomaly through a channel carrying only part of it. Reported in full
  rather than swapped for a metric this signal happens to pass.

==============================================================================
MINIMAL REPAIR SET -- how many channels carry the anomaly?
==============================================================================
Exploratory all-split capture analysis (includes TRAIN); not held-out validation.
182 flagged windows from real labelled MPU6050 hardware. For each k, the
BEST k-of-5 channel subset is repaired to its training mean and the window rescored.

   channels repaired |  flipped to >= 0.5 |  median error after
  --------------------------------------------------------------
     none (baseline) |        0/182       |            24331.72
         best 1 of 5 |        0/182       |             6332.64
         best 2 of 5 |       11/182       |              252.36
         best 3 of 5 |      179/182       |                0.50
         best 4 of 5 |      182/182       |                0.09

  Baseline median error 24331.72; a flip requires error <= 4.13.

  RANK-AWARE LEVEL-2 (proposed metric): 179/182 (98%) recover when the anomaly's MINIMAL SUFFICIENT channel
  set (rank 3) is repaired, vs MEETS the same >= 70% target the single-channel test is held to.
  MEETS the target.
  This is NOT the single-channel number relaxed until it passes: applied to
  gnn_score -- whose anomaly is genuinely single-source -- the rank-aware test
  still passes at rank 1 (see its 100% single-channel result above). The
  instrument's rank is matched to the anomaly's rank; single-source anomalies
  are unaffected. Most common minimal repair sets:
    crest_factor, peak, rms  (175/179)
    crest_factor, kurtosis, peak  (4/179)

  READ THIS TOGETHER WITH THE SINGLE-CHANNEL NUMBER ABOVE, NOT INSTEAD OF IT.
  The single-channel test is the comparable one and its result stands. What this
  adds is the reason it fails: a rank-1 repair cannot undo a rank-k anomaly. An
  impulsive mechanical shock moves rms, peak, crest_factor and kurtosis together
  because they are all functions of the same spike, so no single channel carries
  enough of it. gnn_score passes the single-channel test at 100% precisely because
  its anomaly IS single-source -- a neighbour's evidence. The metric is measuring
  channel correlation, and only incidentally explainability.
